{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_013qwf8huwSDRAsYXJjucoWa", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I have the skill content from the eval source directory. Now proceeding with the full enhancement pipeline.\n\n### Steps Executed\n- Step 1: Input Handling \u2014 completed (file read from eval source at `/Users/rhuss/Development/context-engineering/cc-skill/eval/skill-enhance/cases/case-002-present-to-strong/target-skill/SKILL.md`)\n- Step 2: Load Knowledge \u2014 completed\n- Step 3: Baseline Evaluation \u2014 completed, 0 strong / 9 present / 3 absent / 2 N/A\n- Step 4: Already-Optimal Detection \u2014 not optimal, continued\n- Step 5: Enhancement Targeting \u2014 completed, 11 targets selected (3 absent, 8 present)\n- Step 6: Rewrite \u2014 completed\n- Step 7: File Splitting \u2014 skipped (under 500 lines)\n- Step 8: Prompt Plugin Delegation \u2014 skipped (no `prompt:` prefix skills available)\n- Step 9: Changelog \u2014 in progress\n\n## Baseline Evaluation\n\n| # | Pattern | Category | Status |\n|---|---------|----------|--------|\n| 1 | Activation Metadata | Discovery | present |\n| 2 | Exclusion Clause | Discovery | present |\n| 3 | Context Budget | Context Economy | present |\n| 4 | Progressive Disclosure | Context Economy | N/A |\n| 5 | Control Tuning | Instruction Calibration | present |\n| 6 | Explain-the-Why | Instruction Calibration | absent |\n| 7 | Template Scaffold | Instruction Calibration | present |\n| 8 | In-Skill Examples | Instruction Calibration | present |\n| 9 | Known Gotchas | Instruction Calibration | absent |\n| 10 | Execution Checklist | Workflow Control | present |\n| 11 | Self-Correcting Loop | Workflow Control | absent |\n| 12 | Plan-Validate-Execute | Workflow Control | present |\n| 13 | Utility Bundle | Executable Code | N/A |\n| 14 | Autonomy Calibration | Meta | present |\n\n## Enhanced Skill\n\n```markdown\n---\nname: \"api:scaffold\"\ndescription: \"Generate a REST API endpoint with handler, route, and basic tests. Use when the user asks to scaffold, create, add, or generate a new REST API endpoint, route, or resource. Do NOT use for modifying existing endpoints, adding middleware, or non-REST interfaces (GraphQL, WebSocket, gRPC).\"\nargument-hint: \"<resource-name> [--method GET|POST|PUT|DELETE]\"\nallowed-tools: [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Bash\"]\nuser-invocable: true\n---\n\n# API Endpoint Scaffolder\n\nCreate a new REST API endpoint with handler function, route registration, request/response types, and a basic test file.\n\n**Example**: `api:scaffold orders --method POST` creates a handler, route, request/response types, and test file for `POST /orders`, following the project's existing conventions.\n\n## Steps\n\n### Step 1: Detect Project Framework\n\nRead the project's main dependency file (`package.json`, `go.mod`, `requirements.txt`, or `pyproject.toml`) to determine the web framework:\n\n- Express.js / Fastify / Koa (Node.js)\n- Gin / Chi / Echo (Go)\n- FastAPI / Flask / Django (Python)\n\nIf the framework cannot be determined from the dependency file, check for framework-specific files (e.g., `manage.py` for Django, `main.go` imports). If still unclear, ask the user.\n\n**Completion check**: Framework identified. If not, stop and ask the user.\n\n### Step 2: Survey Existing Patterns\n\nRead up to 2 existing handler files to extract the project's conventions for:\n\n- File naming and directory structure\n- Import style and ordering\n- Error handling patterns (status codes, error response format)\n- Response format (JSON shape, envelope pattern)\n\nLimit reads to handler files only, not the entire codebase. This keeps context focused and avoids loading unrelated code.\n\n### Step 3: Preview the Plan\n\nBefore creating any files, present the user with:\n\n- Files to be created (full paths and purpose)\n- The HTTP method and route path\n- Which existing handler was used as the pattern source\n\nWait for user approval before proceeding. This preview prevents accidental overwrites and lets the user catch naming or path issues early.\n\n### Step 4: Generate Handler\n\nCreate the handler file following the conventions from Step 2.\n\nA common mistake is generating a handler that catches all errors silently and returns 200 with an error message in the body. Match the error handling pattern from existing handlers instead of inventing a new one.\n\n### Step 5: Register Route\n\nAdd the route to the project's router configuration, matching the existing registration style.\n\nCheck that the route path does not conflict with existing routes. A `/users/:id` route added after `/users/search` can shadow the search endpoint in some frameworks.\n\n### Step 6: Create Types\n\nDefine request and response types/structs for the endpoint. Place them in the same location as existing types (a shared `types/` directory, inline in the handler, or a models file).\n\n### Step 7: Generate Test\n\nCreate a basic test file covering:\n\n- Happy path (200/201 response with expected body shape)\n- Invalid input (400 response)\n- Not found (404 response)\n\nExample test structure for a `users` resource with POST method:\n\n```\ndescribe(\"POST /users\", () => {\n  test(\"creates user with valid input \u2192 201\")\n  test(\"rejects missing required fields \u2192 400\")\n  test(\"rejects duplicate email \u2192 409\")\n})\n```\n\n### Step 8: Validate\n\nRun the project's test suite to confirm the new endpoint works and existing tests still pass.\n\nIf tests fail, read the failure output and fix the generated code. Retry up to 2 times. If tests still fail after retries, report the remaining failures to the user and stop.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Generated code follows existing project conventions, which reduces review friction and keeps the codebase consistent.\n- Complex business logic should be added manually after scaffolding. The scaffold provides the wiring (route, handler, types, tests), not the domain logic.\n```\n\n---\n\n## What Changed\n\n| Pattern Applied | What was added/changed | Why |\n|----------------|----------------------|-----|\n| Activation Metadata | Description expanded with trigger verbs (\"scaffold, create, add, generate\") and exclusion phrases | Original description \"Generate a REST API endpoint\" was a single phrasing; routing could miss \"scaffold\" or \"add endpoint\" requests |\n| Exclusion Clause | Added \"Do NOT use for modifying existing endpoints, adding middleware, or non-REST interfaces (GraphQL, WebSocket, gRPC)\" to description | No exclusion existed, risking misactivation for GraphQL schema changes or existing-endpoint modifications |\n| Context Budget | Step 2 limits reads to \"up to 2 existing handler files\" with explicit \"Limit reads to handler files only\" instruction | Original \"Look at existing handlers\" was unbounded and could consume context reading dozens of files |\n| Control Tuning | Step 3 is a rigid approval gate; Step 2 uses flexible convention-following; Step 8 has rigid retry limits (2 max) | All original steps were at the same specificity level with no distinction between exact requirements and judgment calls |\n| Explain-the-Why | Added rationale to Step 2 (\"keeps context focused\"), Step 3 (\"prevents accidental overwrites\"), and Notes (\"reduces review friction\") | Original steps were bare imperatives (\"Create the handler file\") without reasoning for constraints or ordering |\n| Template Scaffold | Step 7 includes a concrete test structure showing `describe`/`test` blocks for a POST /users endpoint | Original listed test categories (\"happy path, 400, 404\") without showing what the generated output looks like |\n| Known Gotchas | Step 4: warning about silent error-catching handlers. Step 5: warning about route path shadowing (`/users/:id` vs `/users/search`) | API scaffolding has well-known framework pitfalls that trip up code generators, and neither was mentioned |\n| Execution Checklist | Step 1 has a completion check (\"Framework identified. If not, stop\"). Step 3 is an explicit gate. Step 8 has retry limits | Original steps were numbered but had no completion criteria, halt conditions, or explicit gates |\n| Self-Correcting Loop | Step 8 mandates test validation, a retry loop (up to 2 attempts), and clear failure reporting to the user | Original Step 6 said \"confirm tests pass\" but had no recovery path or retry limit if they failed |\n| Plan-Validate-Execute | Step 3 previews files to create, route path, and pattern source, then waits for user approval before any writes | Original jumped from framework detection straight to file creation with no preview or confirmation |\n| Autonomy Calibration | Step 3 approval gate matches the file-creation risk profile, combined with existing `allowed-tools` restriction | Original had tool restrictions but no user approval gate despite creating multiple files in the project |\n\n## Status Change\n\n| Pattern | Before | After |\n|---------|--------|-------|\n| 1. Activation Metadata | present | strong |\n| 2. Exclusion Clause | present | strong |\n| 3. Context Budget | present | strong |\n| 5. Control Tuning | present | strong |\n| 6. Explain-the-Why | absent | strong |\n| 7. Template Scaffold | present | strong |\n| 9. Known Gotchas | absent | strong |\n| 10. Execution Checklist | present | strong |\n| 11. Self-Correcting Loop | absent | strong |\n| 12. Plan-Validate-Execute | present | strong |\n| 14. Autonomy Calibration | present | strong |\n\n**Summary**: 11 patterns improved (3 absent \u2192 strong, 8 present \u2192 strong), 0 new files proposed"}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 5057, "cache_read_input_tokens": 65214, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 5057, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "a49218c7-0adc-46b4-ad